With ideal <thermodynamic activities> $a_i=[i]/c^\circ$, the dimensionless <acid dissociation constant> is
$$
\boxed{K_a=\frac{a_{\mathrm{H^+}}a_{\mathrm{HN_2O_2^-}}}{a_{\mathrm{H_2N_2O_2}}}
=\frac{[\mathrm{H^+}][\mathrm{HN_2O_2^-}]}{[\mathrm{H_2N_2O_2}]c^\circ},\qquad c^\circ=1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}.}
$$
The factor $c^\circ$ makes the <equilibrium constant> compatible with $\Delta_rG^\circ=-RT\ln K_a$. If the concentration quotient without $c^\circ$ is used instead, it has concentration units and its numerical value is identical only when the standard concentration is expressed as $1\,\mathrm M$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <standard Gibbs energy of reaction> gives
$$
\ln K_a=-\frac{\Delta_rG^\circ}{RT}
=-\frac{41133}{8.3145(298)}=-16.6011,
\qquad\boxed{K_a=6.17\times10^{-8}.}
$$
The positive <standard Gibbs energy of reaction> means that dissociation is unfavourable when every <thermodynamic activity> equals one; it does not forbid partial dissociation. The corresponding $\mathrm pK_a=-\log_{10}K_a$ is about $7.21$.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**The equilibrium molecular-acid concentration is $\boxed{[\mathrm{H_2N_2O_2}]=0.162\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}}$.** From the supplied <pH> convention,
$$
[\mathrm{H^+}]=c^\circ10^{-4}=10^{-4}\,\mathrm M.
$$
With only these monovalent <ions> appreciably present, <electroneutrality> requires $[\mathrm{HN_2O_2^-}]=[\mathrm{H^+}]$. Substitution in the <acid dissociation constant> gives
$$
[\mathrm{H_2N_2O_2}]
=\frac{(10^{-4}\,\mathrm M)^2}{(6.1690\times10^{-8})(1\,\mathrm M)}
=0.16210\,\mathrm M.
$$
This calculation assumes ideal <thermodynamic activities> for the acid and its conjugate base as well as for $\mathrm H^+$, negligible water <autoionization>, negligible second dissociation, and no additional electrolyte. The acid's analytical concentration would include its dissociated fraction and be approximately $0.16220\,\mathrm M$, rather than exactly the molecular-acid concentration requested.

